<div align="center"><small><a href="tutorials.html">back to tutorials index</a></small></div>

This document explains how to build QuantLibXL and its prerequisites from
source code.

If you want a binary installation of the precompiled addin, go instead to the
<a href="http://www.quantlibxl.org">QuantLibXL</a> web site.

For other tutorials which explain how to compile %QuantLibAddin for platforms
other than Microsoft Excel on Windows, return to the \ref tutorials index.

%QuantLibXL can be built in either of two ways:

\li the hand-maintained <b>Visual Studio</b> solution files, which run on Windows
and build the C++ libraries and the Excel XLL; and
\li the <b>cmake</b> build, which on Windows builds the same libraries and XLL.
The cmake build is x64 only.

\ref build_qlxl_builds \n
\ref build_qlxl_prerequisites \n
\ref build_qlxl_directories \n
\ref build_qlxl_source \n
\ref build_qlxl_vs \n
\ref build_qlxl_cmake \n

\section build_qlxl_builds 1 Choose a Build - Basic or Full

This tutorial offers a choice of two builds as summarized below.  The choice
applies to both the Visual Studio and the cmake build.

<table>
<tr><td><b>Build</b></td><td><b>Description</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Full</td>
<td>This build performs autogeneration of QuantLibXL source code.  Requires you
to install Python; gensrc is included in the source archive.  You can
enhance/customize this build by editing addin XML metadata and re-running
gensrc.</td>
</tr>
<tr><td>Basic</td>
<td>For this build, the autogenerated source code has already been created for
you, and is not recreated during the build.  Does not require Python.
To customize/enhance this build you would need manually to edit the
autogenerated C++ source files.</td>
</tr>
</table>

\section build_qlxl_prerequisites 2 Prerequisites

Before proceeding to compile the source code, consult the documents below.

\subsection build_qlxl_conventions 2.1 Conventions

See the following document regarding naming conventions in use for the project:
\ref conventions

\subsection build_qlxl_boost 2.2 Boost

%QuantLibXL depends on Boost.  You will need the compiled Boost libraries, the
Boost header files alone are not sufficient.  The build requires Boost 1.58 or
later; this release was tested using Boost 1.83.

\subsection build_qlxl_python 2.3 Python 3.x

For the <b>Full</b> build, some code is autogenerated by gensrc, which is a
Python script.  Before rebuilding %QuantLibXL you need to have Python 3.x
installed on your machine.  Here is the link to the Python website:
<a href="http://www.python.org/">http://www.python.org/</a>.

For the cmake build, gensrc is invoked automatically when you configure with
<tt>-DRUN_GENSRC=ON</tt> (see \ref build_qlxl_cmake), and cmake locates the
Python interpreter for you.

For the Visual Studio <b>Full</b> build, if Python is installed on your machine
so that .py files are associated with the Python executable, then the gensrc
scripts should build OK with no modification required.

Otherwise, you might need to edit these files...

\verbatim
    ObjectHandler\gensrc\Makefile.vc
    QuantLibAddin\gensrc\Makefile.vc
\endverbatim

...and set the PYTHON variable equal to the location of the Python executable
on your machine, e.g:

\verbatim
    PYTHON=C:\Program Files\Python312\python.exe
\endverbatim

\section build_qlxl_directories 3 A Note on Directories

You are now ready to proceed with the build.  The source code is distributed as
two downloads:
\li a single combined archive containing %QuantLibXL together with its sibling
    projects gensrc, ObjectHandler and %QuantLibAddin; and
\li <a href="http://quantlib.org/">QuantLib</a> 1.42.0, the core C++ analytics
    library, which is released separately.

The build refers to the prerequisite projects %QuantLib, ObjectHandler,
%QuantLibAddin, and (for the Full build) gensrc using relative pathnames.  In
order for this to work, these projects must reside side by side in the directory
tree, and the name of each project's parent directory must not include the
version number (e.g. \c %QuantLib not \c QuantLib-1.42.0).  The combined archive
already contains gensrc, ObjectHandler, %QuantLibAddin and %QuantLibXL in the
correct layout, so you only need to add %QuantLib alongside them.

Create a new empty folder on your hard drive to serve as the root directory for
the build.  You can use any path; for purposes of this tutorial the build
directory is <tt>C:\\build_ql_1_42_0</tt>.  Unpack the combined archive into that
directory; it expands to a single top-level <tt>%QuantLibAddin</tt> folder
containing the bundled projects.  Then unpack %QuantLib alongside them and
manually rename its target directory, removing the \c 1.42.0 suffix.  After all
of the source code is unpacked you should have the following directory structure:
\verbatim
C:\build_ql_1_42_0\QuantLibAddin\gensrc (required only for the Full build)
C:\build_ql_1_42_0\QuantLibAddin\ObjectHandler
C:\build_ql_1_42_0\QuantLibAddin\QuantLib
C:\build_ql_1_42_0\QuantLibAddin\QuantLibAddin
C:\build_ql_1_42_0\QuantLibAddin\QuantLibXL
\endverbatim

\section build_qlxl_source 4 Install the Source Code

Download the combined source-code archive from:

<tt>&lt;download-url&gt;</tt>

and uncompress it to <tt>C:\\build_ql_1_42_0</tt>.  This single archive contains
gensrc, ObjectHandler, %QuantLibAddin and %QuantLibXL, already laid out side by
side under a top-level <tt>%QuantLibAddin</tt> folder; no separate per-project
download of those projects is required.

Then go to the
<a href="https://github.com/lballabio/QuantLib/releases/tag/QuantLib-v1.42">QuantLib downloads page</a>,
download <tt>QuantLib-1.42.0.zip</tt> and uncompress it alongside the bundled
projects, renaming the target directory to
<tt>C:\\build_ql_1_42_0\\%QuantLibAddin\\%QuantLib</tt>.

\section build_qlxl_vs 5 Build QuantLibXL with Visual Studio

The hand-maintained Visual Studio solutions select the platform toolset
automatically from the version of Visual Studio used to open the solution (VS
2022 / v143 and VS 2026 / v145 are supported), so there is no longer a separate
solution file per compiler version.

<b>Basic</b> build - open solution
<tt>QuantLibXL\\QuantLibXL_basic.sln</tt>.

<b>Full</b> build - open solution
<tt>QuantLibXL\\QuantLibXL_full.sln</tt>.

Choose configuration <tt>Release (static runtime)</tt> and platform <tt>x64</tt>
(<tt>Win32</tt> is also supported), then do <tt>Build | Build Solution</tt>.

The build process creates the %QuantLibXL XLL file
<tt>QuantLibXL\\xll\\QuantLibXL-v145-x64-mt-s-1_42_0.xll</tt> (the exact name
reflects your toolset and platform).  Instructions for loading the addin are
provided in the QuantLibXL installation document:
<a href="http://www.quantlibxl.org/installation.html">http://www.quantlibxl.org/installation.html</a>

\section build_qlxl_cmake 6 Build QuantLibXL with CMake

The cmake build is x64 only.  It builds each project into its own build tree, so
you build the prerequisites first and then point the %QuantLibXL build at them.
To see the presets available on your platform, run <tt>cmake --list-presets</tt>;
the example below uses the <tt>windows-vs2026-x64-static</tt> preset.  The
<tt>-DRUN_GENSRC=ON</tt> option selects the <b>Full</b> build (it runs gensrc to
regenerate the autogenerated source code and requires Python 3); omit it for a
<b>Basic</b> build.

Build %QuantLib, ObjectHandler and %QuantLibAddin first (see \ref build_cpp for
the addin libraries), then configure and build the XLL:

\verbatim
cd C:\build_ql_1_42_0\QuantLibAddin\QuantLibXL
cmake --preset windows-vs2026-x64-static -DRUN_GENSRC=ON ^
    "-DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=../QuantLib/build/windows-vs2026-x64-static;../ObjectHandler/build/windows-vs2026-x64-static;../QuantLibAddin/build/windows-vs2026-x64-static"
cmake --build build\windows-vs2026-x64-static --config Release --target QuantLibXL
\endverbatim

The build produces the same %QuantLibXL XLL described above.  Instructions for
loading the addin are provided in the QuantLibXL installation document:
<a href="http://www.quantlibxl.org/installation.html">http://www.quantlibxl.org/installation.html</a>

<div align="center"><small><a href="tutorials.html">back to tutorials index</a></small></div>

